[{:seon.task/name "a-vanished-file-is-a-fact"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23: the standing root's watcher died, seon.watch/died: its batch handler threw .../docs/references.md (No such file or directory): root no longer watches the checkout (exit 143). A git merge or bin/update removed the file for a moment while the batch read the wiki: seon.docs/pages listed docs/ and then slurped each file, and the one gone in between threw. Correct, at the producer (root's ruling): one read of a directory's files, seon.lib/dir-texts, which seon.docs/pages and my.root/task-files both call, leaves out a file listed and gone when read, as a deleted file is: the wiki's load retracts that page's sections, a task file left out is not judged (seon.task/load-tx: a file not given is not judged, so its tasks are never cancelled by a moment's absence), and the change that removed it or writes it back makes the next batch. The catch is FileNotFoundException only, named in docs/failure-points.md. Folds the two directory readers the one-offs survey found (notes/one-offs-2026-10-23.md, item 6)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.watch-test/a-file-gone-when-its-batch-reads-it-is-a-change}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-watchers-end-waits-on-its-own-exit"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (notes/stability-2026-10-25.md; the owner: platform stability first). Weak: src/seon/watch.clj:58-59 (died) runs .waitFor and slurps stderr on a child that closed stdout but is still alive, and src/seon/watch.clj:90 (stop!) .joins an edge thread that may be parked in on-batch. Correct: the watcher's end is its exit (kill then waitFor, the exit a fact), and the edge thread ends on its stream's EOF, which that kill gives. Proof: a watcher whose child closes stdout and stays alive, and a stop during a parked on-batch, both end."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "root-watches-what-its-loaders-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-26 (root's question: fseventsd, pid 81742, has held a core since about 10-23). Measured, read-only: fseventsd uses ~62 CPU-seconds a minute at ~127 file events a second system-wide (a 20 s FSEvents stream on /: 2,540 events), about 8 ms of its CPU an event, so it is saturated: today's watcher runs measured deliveries 5 to 18 s late under load. Its cumulative CPU (6,566 min in 12.4 days since its start on 10-14, 37% on average) means the pinned stretch began around 10-22/23, which is when seon.watch arrived (3565b72e45, 10-23), not when fseventsd started. What Seon asks of it: my.root/watch! watches the whole checkout and the repository's refs with per-file events (native/watch.c: FileEvents, NoDefer, WatchRoot, latency 0). For the main tree that checkout holds every worktree (.worktrees/*), each with target/ (builds, shards, check runs, lmdb-sem's 65,536-file search), runs/ (LMDB stores) and .git/modules: of the 20 s sample, ~85% of the volume's events were under /Users/sean/src/seon-next, and every one is delivered to the main root's stream, though watch-loads reads none of them (a batch no loader reads writes nothing). Every test root and watch test starts a stream of its own over its checkout too: 9,673 new fseventsd clients in the 6 hours to 20:30 (3,454 and 4,308 in the two busiest hours, much of it this session's watcher loops), each, since d28e10a585, starting from an event id, so fseventsd runs a history scan (scan_old) for each. Not proven: how fseventsd's per-event cost depends on its clients (removed clients are not logged, and its client list needs root), so whether stale clients add to it is open. Correct, at the cause: root watches what its loaders read, my.root/loader-dirs (docs, the task dir, the source dirs) and the refs, never the checkout, so the churn of builds, stores and other worktrees never reaches a stream; watch-loads' :rescan and :root handling is unchanged (a rescan above a loader dir still reads it). Measured before and after, on the same machine: fseventsd's CPU a minute and the events delivered to the main root's stream a minute (its :seon.root/watch fact's :paths), and a watcher's ready-to-delivery lag. The owner decides whether fseventsd is restarted for a clean baseline. Replaces nothing; deletes the checkout root from watch!."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.watch-test/a-change-arrives-and-a-dead-watcher-is-a-platform-error}}]
